3
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:19,030
[music]

4
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:23,430
Hi everyone, welcome to today's episode of Software Engineering Radio.

5
00:00:23,430 --> 00:00:32,470
Our guest is Mark Brooker, VPN Distinguished Engineer at AWS, and our topic is spec-driven development.

6
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:42,670
If you tried to use AI to write code, you've probably noticed how productive AI coding can be, but also that things start to break pretty soon.

7
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:45,350
Spec-driven development aims to correct that.

8
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:55,670
So, rather than treating prompts as a main artifact, it treats specs and intent as a source of truth and uses them to drive the code, the tests, and reviews.

9
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:57,990
Welcome to the show today, Mark.

10
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:00,230
So great to have you here.

11
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:04,470
Is there anything you'd like to add either to the topic or your bio before we get started?

12
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:06,350
Yeah, well, thank you.

13
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:07,990
Thanks for this opportunity to talk about this topic.

14
00:01:07,990 --> 00:01:14,670
I've been building software in various ways for the best part of 30 years, maybe slightly over 30 years,

15
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:26,110
and this is so exciting to have this new set of tools in my toolbox as a software engineer and as somebody who's really interested in building software and more broadly building systems to serve customers with software.

16
00:01:26,110 --> 00:01:33,550
So, you said you've been doing this, developing software in different form or fashion for a long time.

17
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:37,910
Could you help us by summarizing the current SDLC best practices?

18
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:51,870
Yeah, well, I think obviously that varies from organization to organization and team to team, but the way that I see that is when I think about a high-performing software team, this is a team that is iterating quickly.

19
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:55,630
They're very close to the customer in gathering requirements.

20
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:57,670
They're very close to the business in gathering requirements.

21
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:04,910
They're working to get feedback from the business and from the customer about what they're building as quickly as possible.

22
00:02:04,910 --> 00:02:11,070
Often they are operationally close to running the things that they are building, especially if they're cloud services or software as a service,

23
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:22,070
and they are working closely across multiple disciplines between software teams, designers, product folks, customer-facing folks, and so on.

24
00:02:22,070 --> 00:02:26,230
And we can talk about get into the more kind of software development part of that,

25
00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:38,310
but for me it is really about structuring teams and organizations to be able to iterate quickly and be close to their customers and close to their businesses that lead to good outcomes for software development.

26
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:46,990
So, you mentioned a lot about iteration and feedback loops and structuring teams.

27
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:52,830
So, so far a lot of teams have been aligned with the Scrum or similar form of iterative dev process.

28
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:59,870
As we start to introduce AI into the mix, what are the things that you have been observing?

29
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:09,310
Probably the biggest thing that's changing is the multiplier of overhead of process goes up.

30
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:11,430
It becomes so much more of a big impact, right?

31
00:03:11,430 --> 00:03:19,710
And so, if I think about, hey, I used to be spending 70% of my time building software, actually just writing code and testing code,

32
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:31,190
writing designs, and so on, and 30% of time on overhead, if the speed of the software building portion has gone up by 2x or 4x, let's say 2x, right?

33
00:03:31,190 --> 00:03:38,190
Now, I've suddenly shifted the ratio from 70/30 to something that's closer to 50/50.

34
00:03:38,190 --> 00:03:43,510
And so, as that software portion speeds up or the code building portion speeds up,

35
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:53,110
so we also have to figure out how to speed up the rest of the software development process, or we don't get a ton of the benefit.

36
00:03:53,110 --> 00:04:01,990
And so, that's been this kind of second-order effect I've seen with teams as they have embraced AI and embraced the faster speed of coding that

37
00:04:01,990 --> 00:04:06,110
have gone with that is they've also had to pay a bunch of attention to how do we build better processes?

38
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:08,390
How do we make those processes lightweight?

39
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:12,830
How do we use AI to accelerate a lot of those processes?

40
00:04:12,830 --> 00:04:17,229
And that's something that there's a ton of conversation around now that I'm excited about.

41
00:04:17,229 --> 00:04:19,630
Then, I think the other part of that

42
00:04:19,630 --> 00:04:31,670
is another big enabler in taking advantage of the speed of being able to build with AI is having great infrastructure for testing and for knowing if that code works.

43
00:04:31,670 --> 00:04:41,550
And so, the teams that I've seen get the best benefit from AI are the teams who have made investments in test infrastructure,

44
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:48,350
in testing, in knowing what their software should do, and therefore can move really fast and move really fast with confidence.

45
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:56,110
You spent a lot of time in focusing on all the things that surround the actual writing of the code.

46
00:04:56,110 --> 00:05:03,258
Though a lot of the hype in the last year has been just on the coding with wipe coding being a term that nobody could not have

47
00:05:03,268 --> 00:05:03,390
[laughter]

48
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:06,871
heard of. Yeah.

49
00:05:06,871 --> 00:05:09,030
How did you make that journey or

50
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:19,150
how did you observe developers making the journey from wipe coding to now understanding that's a small part or not the whole part of the whole experience?

51
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:22,310
I think that's something of a natural journey.

52
00:05:22,310 --> 00:05:24,310
And so, I start off wipe coding and it feels great, right?

53
00:05:24,310 --> 00:05:32,110
Like it feels so productive and I get so much done and I generate so much code, and then I realize well, I've actually got to get this out into production.

54
00:05:32,110 --> 00:05:37,630
You know, if I want to have an impact on my business and my customers, I've got to get it out into production.

55
00:05:37,630 --> 00:05:39,230
Oh, now I need to be able to test it.

56
00:05:39,230 --> 00:05:40,830
Now I need to be able to validate it.

57
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:47,350
Now I need to actually be able to know that those features that I built are the right features for the customer.

58
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:51,231
So, it's this natural thing that happens to teams of saying, hey, we're going to pick up these wipe coding tools. It's going to feel great.

59
00:05:51,231 --> 00:05:53,070
We're going to feel super productive.

60
00:05:53,070 --> 00:05:54,910
And then the sort of and then happens.

61
00:05:54,910 --> 00:05:59,230
It's like, and then, well, I need to maintain this piece of software.

62
00:05:59,230 --> 00:06:01,190
I need to test this piece of software.

63
00:06:01,190 --> 00:06:07,030
I need to listen to customers and feedback in what they're saying and improve the piece of software.

64
00:06:07,030 --> 00:06:18,750
And that's where it becomes really obvious that wipe coding, as great as it feels and as useful a tool as it can be, isn't everything and doesn't solve the end-to-end software life cycle.

65
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:24,390
It solves a small part of that or accelerates a small part of that.

66
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:34,110
And even if you accelerate that substantially, you haven't yet accelerated the whole end-to-end process of what it takes to build a successful software system.

67
00:06:34,110 --> 00:06:39,350
But if I'm building something for my own use, little hobby project, that's okay, right?

68
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:45,630
It's fine and I get a huge amount of the whole end-to-end life cycle done just with wipe coding.

69
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:57,430
If I'm building a piece of a critical service or an application that I need to put in front of my customers and meet a safety bar and a security bar and a quality bar, well, then I need something more.

70
00:06:57,430 --> 00:07:05,950
And even more so, over the long term, if I need to maintain that software for weeks, months,

71
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:11,870
years, decades, then I need a more, let's say, sustainable process than the one that this kind of pure wipe coding approach offers me.

72
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:16,550
You mentioned maintaining a couple of times and that's key.

73
00:07:16,550 --> 00:07:18,950
But what about even reading the code before that?

74
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:20,910
How does that work?

75
00:07:20,910 --> 00:07:22,990
How do code reviews work?

76
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:30,470
Yeah, I think this is one of the key questions that's being asked in the industry right now is where do we go with code reading?

77
00:07:30,470 --> 00:07:32,310
Where do we go with code reviews?

78
00:07:32,310 --> 00:07:43,310
Code review from my perspective has historically been this incredibly useful tool for driving software quality, for driving alignment, for driving API quality.

79
00:07:43,310 --> 00:07:49,510
I'm not super optimistic about it being a way of preventing bugs, but I think it does drive out quality.

80
00:07:49,510 --> 00:07:52,390
It does improve code design and has these good outcomes.

81
00:07:52,390 --> 00:07:57,150
But as we accelerate, well, what is the role of that kind of code reading?

82
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:06,230
And I suspect where we're going to end up with for a lot of code bases is, and not all,

83
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:12,910
but a lot, is code reading is going to go a little bit of the same way that reading assembly code did 50 years ago.

84
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:21,310
We're going to start building in these high-level languages and people are going to say, well, if you're going to build in a high-level language, you have to read the compiler output and check it's right.

85
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:23,111
And people did that, right? They did that a long time ago.

86
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:34,589
And then over time, we stopped doing that because it just didn't feel like a good use of our time, and we felt that reviewing the high-level language was much more useful and much more productive and led to better outcome.

87
00:08:34,599 --> 00:08:44,950
And I think that's more or less where we're going to end up with AI-powered software building is where we're going to look at a lot of the code that gets generated almost like it is this assembly code, right?

88
00:08:44,950 --> 00:08:54,510
It is an implementation detail, and we want to be reviewing and paying attention to is the specification, right?

89
00:08:54,510 --> 00:08:59,710
Is the writing of what should the software do and how are we checking it does that?

90
00:08:59,710 --> 00:09:10,230
That's key in terms of spec-driven development is how are we checking what the software is supposed to do and how did we provide that in the first place?

91
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:12,070
Is that the key goal?

92
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:18,270
Like, can you maybe take a step back now and explain what is spec-driven development from your perspective?

93
00:09:18,270 --> 00:09:21,350
Yeah, good to take a step back on that, for sure.

94
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:27,910
I think, you know, as long as we've been building software as an organizational thing, right?

95
00:09:27,910 --> 00:09:33,510
As a team sport, part of that has been figuring out what should the software do and writing that down.

96
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:45,310
And sometimes we've written that down as formal documents, sometimes we've written that down as a series of tickets, sometimes we've written that down as a bunch of napkin sketches and some stuff on a whiteboard.

97
00:09:45,310 --> 00:09:47,790
And all of those things are specifications.

98
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:57,390
And I think in this new era of AI, what we're finding is that writing down what your software can do, and And going to call it this word specification.

99
00:09:57,390 --> 00:10:02,390
That implies a level of formality that doesn't necessarily require,

100
00:10:02,390 --> 00:10:12,190
but it is a description that is as crisp as it needs to be about what the software can do and what a successful piece of software looks like.

101
00:10:12,190 --> 00:10:17,990
And then once we have that artifact, then we can start using that to do two things.

102
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:22,990
One of them is build the software, often with an AI assisted process.

103
00:10:22,990 --> 00:10:31,470
And if you look at the specification-driven development flow in something like Kiro, this is an agentic process that takes that specification, starts building it with code.

104
00:10:31,470 --> 00:10:35,830
Then the other thing that we can do with that specification is build tests.

105
00:10:35,830 --> 00:10:39,710
And this is where techniques like property-based testing come in are super powerful.

106
00:10:39,710 --> 00:10:48,550
I can extract properties from that specification and build tests that assert that those properties are true about the piece of code that I've built.

107
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:54,030
But crucially, that specification isn't a static artifact.

108
00:10:54,030 --> 00:11:04,751
As I build the code, as I test the code, as I talk to customers, as I talk to my business, as I listen to the various stakeholders, I'm going to keep improving that specification. I'm going to clarify it.

109
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:07,950
Maybe I'm going to generalize it in some ways.

110
00:11:07,950 --> 00:11:10,150
Maybe I'm going to make some pieces of it much more specific.

111
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:16,910
Maybe I'm going to add in some code snippets or UI marks and improve that specification.

112
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:22,829
And then that is going to feed into improving the code and improving the tests.

113
00:11:22,829 --> 00:11:28,790
And so this is a iterative software development approach, a short-term iterative software development approach,

114
00:11:28,790 --> 00:11:39,630
but one that is very explicit about writing down the goals of what it is that we're trying to build and then really using AI tools to accelerate the building of that.

115
00:11:39,630 --> 00:11:42,230
So you mentioned Kiro.

116
00:11:42,230 --> 00:11:44,510
Can you just talk to our audience about what Kiro is?

117
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:49,550
Yeah, so Kiro is essentially three things.

118
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:52,870
It's an IDE, it's a CLI, a command line interface, and most importantly,

119
00:11:52,870 --> 00:12:01,950
it's a set of agents that we've built here at Amazon that we use for our internal development and we can talk about some of our successes with that and is available to customers too.

120
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:06,510
It's a set of tools for agentic development.

121
00:12:06,510 --> 00:12:13,790
It supports the vibe coding mode, it supports the CLI-based step-by-step mode, but most importantly in my mind,

122
00:12:13,790 --> 00:12:21,990
it supports this specification-driven development approach where alongside the Kiro agent inside the Kiro IDE,

123
00:12:21,990 --> 00:12:28,510
you develop a specification of the software and you use that specification to develop the software,

124
00:12:28,510 --> 00:12:41,110
use that specification to build the tests powered by those Kiro agents, and then that feeds back, then you go out to the world, you show the world your software, and then you feed that back into improving and sharpening that specification.

125
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:46,510
There are a lot of tools out there today.

126
00:12:46,510 --> 00:12:48,750
So I'm just curious what led you to build a new one?

127
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:53,390
I think we had two pieces of vision there.

128
00:12:53,390 --> 00:13:02,110
One of them was when we started this journey of building Kiro and building the Kiro agents, the world was starting to move on from the kind of using AI as smart autocomplete,

129
00:13:02,110 --> 00:13:09,190
which was a nice interim step, to this vibe coding mode of prompt-by-prompt development.

130
00:13:09,190 --> 00:13:11,990
And we had used that internally at Amazon and at AWS.

131
00:13:11,990 --> 00:13:21,910
And what we were finding with that prompt-by-prompt development is it it felt really good early on, but it wasn't scaling super well to more complex codebases.

132
00:13:21,910 --> 00:13:23,950
And most importantly, it wasn't scaling well over time, right?

133
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:32,790
Like we would build software prompt-by-prompt, then we would write a prompt to add a feature and it would go and undo a bunch of the stuff that it had done.

134
00:13:32,790 --> 00:13:35,870
It sort of forgotten requirements from earlier on.

135
00:13:35,870 --> 00:13:43,870
And that got a bunch of us in different ways thinking about what if we could solve that requirement over time problem?

136
00:13:43,870 --> 00:13:50,550
And that's where we ended up with specification-driven development of saying, "Hey, let's write down all of these requirements.

137
00:13:50,550 --> 00:13:52,870
Let's keep all of these requirements handy.

138
00:13:52,870 --> 00:14:00,871
So as we iterate on a piece of software, we can refer back to them and say, 'Does this piece of software still meet these other things that we were doing?'" Right?

139
00:14:00,871 --> 00:14:04,990
It's like having a map rather than having turn-by-turn directions.

140
00:14:04,990 --> 00:14:11,630
So building Kiro allowed us to really experiment with and then really invest in the specification-driven development.

141
00:14:11,630 --> 00:14:13,710
The other thing,

142
00:14:13,710 --> 00:14:20,470
the other investment that we've been making is in code reasoning and

143
00:14:20,470 --> 00:14:29,150
neurosymbolic AI where we use symbolic tools, solvers, and so on, these more formal side of AI to reason about code.

144
00:14:29,150 --> 00:14:34,270
And we wanted to bring the power of those tools to software developers in an easier way too.

145
00:14:34,270 --> 00:14:41,510
And by building those tools into Kiro where they can do things like power some of our work on property-based testing,

146
00:14:41,510 --> 00:14:48,230
we can bring the power of code reasoning tools and symbolic reasoning tools to programmers in a way that

147
00:14:48,230 --> 00:14:55,150
it doesn't require them to be experts in formal methods or automated reasoning and so on.

148
00:14:55,150 --> 00:15:00,310
Could you take an example and walk us through the steps of how somebody would use Kiro?

149
00:15:00,310 --> 00:15:05,190
And I'm really curious about an example of a property-based test that you mentioned a couple of times too.

150
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:07,870
Yeah, let's pick an example.

151
00:15:07,870 --> 00:15:13,351
One of our recent releases, maybe not that recent anymore. We built Aurora DB SQL, right?

152
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:19,110
This great scalable, multi-region active-active SQL database.

153
00:15:19,110 --> 00:15:26,949
And what the team realized that customers needed was a set of drivers to make that database easier to use from within Java.

154
00:15:26,949 --> 00:15:33,390
So we could write a specification that said, "Here's the driver that we need to build.

155
00:15:33,390 --> 00:15:36,030
Here are the properties that it needs to have.

156
00:15:36,030 --> 00:15:37,750
Here are the ways that customers should interact with it."

157
00:15:37,750 --> 00:15:40,470
Sort of an API sketch.

158
00:15:40,470 --> 00:15:45,829
And then use that property, that flow to generate the implementation of that driver.

159
00:15:45,829 --> 00:15:49,829
And then when we think about a property-based test,

160
00:15:49,829 --> 00:15:58,150
a property-based test would take out something from the specification like every connection attempt contains an authorization token.

161
00:15:58,150 --> 00:16:04,430
And then build a test that tests in an automated way lots and

162
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:17,270
lots and lots of permutations of using the driver's API and makes sure that that property of every attempt to connect contains an authorization token is always true no matter what permutations of the APIs.

163
00:16:17,270 --> 00:16:25,230
Either I put things in the API and I get a valid request out or I put things in the API and I get a helpful failure or helpful error message out.

164
00:16:25,230 --> 00:16:26,790
And so that's quite a bit in the small.

165
00:16:26,790 --> 00:16:30,110
It's a relatively modest-sized project, but a very useful one.

166
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:44,750
Another bigger project is that we have been building a new fundamental piece of our inference infrastructure also using Kiro, also using specification-driven development.

167
00:16:44,750 --> 00:16:49,829
And that's more of the kind of cloud service type rather than end driver.

168
00:16:49,829 --> 00:16:59,350
And so there the specification is about this is a cloud service that does these things, has this architecture, it has this API, it's written in Rust,

169
00:16:59,350 --> 00:17:04,590
and so we want to use all safe code for example, and then generate the code from that.

170
00:17:04,590 --> 00:17:12,150
And then we get these specifications or properties again, like if a customer calls this API,

171
00:17:12,150 --> 00:17:20,550
either it passes on the request in this form or routes the request in this form or it gives the customer a useful 400 or 500 error message.

172
00:17:20,550 --> 00:17:28,069
And what the property-based test will do and what makes it so interesting is it isn't going to test five or six cases like a human developer might.

173
00:17:28,069 --> 00:17:37,630
It is in an automated way going to go off and test hundreds, thousands, millions of different API permutations and make sure that property holds in all of them.

174
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:47,110
Can you maybe walk us through the stages potentially for based on some of the things you said that potentially may not be applicable to all projects,

175
00:17:47,110 --> 00:17:57,790
but when I think of the SDLC and from some of the pointers you had, the spec is the first thing, but could you use AI to refine the spec is the question.

176
00:17:57,790 --> 00:18:07,790
Then what about the architecture, documentation, the design documentation, the code gen will follow that, and then talk about code review too.

177
00:18:07,790 --> 00:18:13,630
You did say the generated code, you could start thinking of it really as assembly and not really necessary to read it,

178
00:18:13,630 --> 00:18:20,870
but would you still potentially get a different model perhaps to validate the code by some form of code review?

179
00:18:20,870 --> 00:18:29,550
You mentioned a lot about test, but maybe just walk us through all the stages for a complex project and what would you then not necessarily do for a simpler project?

180
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:35,550
Well, I think step one for every project is figuring out what problem you're trying to solve and for who, right?

181
00:18:35,550 --> 00:18:37,551
Like who is the customer? What do they need?

182
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:41,111
What are the requirements of the business? And AI helps there.

183
00:18:41,111 --> 00:18:43,950
It can help me pull in a bunch of requirements.

184
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:45,710
It can help me write documents.

185
00:18:45,710 --> 00:18:53,630
It can help me figure out what questions I need to ask different stakeholders, but doesn't fundamentally change that step of the software life cycle.

186
00:18:53,630 --> 00:18:55,669
And that's going to vary, right?

187
00:18:55,669 --> 00:19:00,909
If I'm writing a super important piece of code for a new AWS service, I'm going to go wide with that.

188
00:19:00,909 --> 00:19:02,510
I'm going to talk to a bunch of people.

189
00:19:02,510 --> 00:19:04,390
I'm going to really understand the economics of that.

190
00:19:04,390 --> 00:19:09,230
If I'm just hacking on a software project, that's something I'm going to do in my head in a couple of minutes.

191
00:19:09,230 --> 00:19:15,270
And then we have the step of writing down the initial version, let me say, of the specification.

192
00:19:15,270 --> 00:19:21,750
And then yeah, I mean, I can do that manually or I can have the AI help me with that.

193
00:19:21,750 --> 00:19:30,350
That can be I start off with develop a specification based on these prompts, based on these customer use cases, based on these customer anecdotes.

194
00:19:30,350 --> 00:19:33,750
And then I can read that specification and say, "Well, no, that's not quite what I meant.

195
00:19:33,750 --> 00:19:36,149
Let's iterate on that together."

196
00:19:36,149 --> 00:19:43,470
And so that can be anything from me writing it down all at once to kind of vibe specifying, right?

197
00:19:43,470 --> 00:19:45,390
Like, "Hey, I'm going to go piece by piece.

198
00:19:45,390 --> 00:19:49,669
I'm going to go anecdote by anecdote and check that those meet in the specification."

199
00:19:49,669 --> 00:19:51,590
But what's important is that I'm reading that specification.

200
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:53,190
I'm thinking about it.

201
00:19:53,190 --> 00:19:54,870
Is it the right thing?

202
00:19:54,870 --> 00:19:56,390
And it's only an initial specification.

203
00:19:56,390 --> 00:19:58,910
So we're going to close the loop on that in a second.

204
00:19:58,910 --> 00:20:07,630
Then, again working with the AI, I am going to turn that specification into a set of design decisions, right?

205
00:20:07,630 --> 00:20:09,791
Like is this a web service? Is this a microservice?

206
00:20:09,791 --> 00:20:13,071
Is this a library? Is it a UI application?

207
00:20:13,080 --> 00:20:14,871
Is it a web application? Right?

208
00:20:14,871 --> 00:20:24,190
Those are important design choices that again are going to be driven by the needs of the business, needs of the problem I'm trying to solve.

209
00:20:24,190 --> 00:20:26,230
They aren't kinds of decisions that the AI can make for me.

210
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:32,390
They're decisions I'm making based on my understanding of the problem I'm trying to solve.

211
00:20:32,390 --> 00:20:34,430
Then we're going to make a bunch of implementation choices.

212
00:20:34,430 --> 00:20:36,030
I'm going to choose a programming language.

213
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:38,710
Maybe I'm going to choose some frameworks.

214
00:20:38,710 --> 00:20:40,630
Maybe I'm going to choose some libraries, right?

215
00:20:40,630 --> 00:20:44,590
If I'm building a web app, I'm probably going to use TypeScript.

216
00:20:44,590 --> 00:20:47,910
If I'm building a new component for an AWS service, I'm probably going to use Rust.

217
00:20:47,910 --> 00:20:54,750
If I'm building a library for people to consume in Python, I'm probably going to use Python.

218
00:20:54,750 --> 00:20:58,991
If I'm doing some AI science and data analysis, maybe I'm going to use Python again. What frameworks do I want?

219
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,670
Do I want to use internal or external frameworks?

220
00:21:02,670 --> 00:21:04,230
Do I want to use open source things?

221
00:21:04,230 --> 00:21:06,830
So then I've got my specification.

222
00:21:06,830 --> 00:21:08,390
I've got this high-level description of my software design.

223
00:21:08,390 --> 00:21:12,590
And then I've got this low-level description of these implementation choices.

224
00:21:12,590 --> 00:21:19,390
Then I'm going to step into, okay, let me go and generate the code, the first versions of the code, and the first versions of the tests.

225
00:21:19,390 --> 00:21:31,070
And I'm going to do that working with the agent step by step, stepping through the specification, and at each step making sure that I'm moving forward and implementing more and more of the specification as it comes.

226
00:21:31,070 --> 00:21:34,470
And then we get to the crucial part where I've generated the code.

227
00:21:34,470 --> 00:21:38,270
It's passing all of the tests.

228
00:21:38,270 --> 00:21:41,630
And now I'm going to see does it actually meet the needs of the customer or business?

229
00:21:41,630 --> 00:21:45,710
Does it actually do the thing that I'm building this software system to do?

230
00:21:45,710 --> 00:21:50,710
And maybe it does initially, but then people will say, "Well, it'd be really cool if you had a feature to do this thing."

231
00:21:50,710 --> 00:21:52,670
Or be really cool if this button was over there.

232
00:21:52,670 --> 00:21:57,030
Or be really cool if you redesigned this to be clearer in some way.

233
00:21:57,030 --> 00:21:59,191
And then I loop back. I loop back to that specification.

234
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:06,270
I loop back to those design choices, and I iteratively change them, and then we rebuild the code alongside the agent.

235
00:22:06,270 --> 00:22:13,510
We rebuild the tests alongside the agent, and then run the software in production again.

236
00:22:13,510 --> 00:22:21,150
And so it remains this iterative process, but it's an iterative process where the core goal of the iteration is keeping a specification up to date.

237
00:22:21,150 --> 00:22:26,230
And downstream of that is keeping code and tests and so on up to date.

238
00:22:26,240 --> 00:22:38,070
Another benefit from I got from what you said is it gives you an ability to check along the way rather than just handing off the spec to the AI and then getting the final code generated.

239
00:22:38,070 --> 00:22:48,871
You're actually have a checkpoint where you read the design doc that is generated, make sure it's correct, or any details of the spec itself, and make sure it's correct, and so on. Is that right?

240
00:22:48,871 --> 00:22:50,990
Yeah, that's exactly right.

241
00:22:50,990 --> 00:23:02,510
And how long you let the AI run probably varies from application to application and goal to goal, but it does give you this checkpoint of checking in of am I headed in the right direction?

242
00:23:02,510 --> 00:23:04,630
Am I driving to the right place?

243
00:23:04,630 --> 00:23:08,870
So that you get into an autonomous taxi, and you say, "I want to go to this place."

244
00:23:08,870 --> 00:23:12,669
And you watch out the window and see are we actually going there?

245
00:23:12,669 --> 00:23:14,430
Or maybe I've changed my opinion, and I want to go somewhere else.

246
00:23:14,430 --> 00:23:24,270
But yeah, and it does give you these interim steps of, "Okay, before I go off and generate all this code and spend all of this time and spend all of these tokens,

247
00:23:24,280 --> 00:23:28,669
am I actually building something that is going to solve my customers' problems?"

248
00:23:28,679 --> 00:23:34,790
A question about the models that you use.

249
00:23:34,790 --> 00:23:40,550
Is there any benefit that you've observed from maybe using a different model for some of those stages, perhaps for code review?

250
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:42,430
Yeah, so that's really interesting.

251
00:23:42,430 --> 00:23:46,310
So actually we need to talk about the code review question.

252
00:23:46,310 --> 00:23:49,310
I think tentatively yes, right?

253
00:23:49,310 --> 00:23:57,430
It certainly is useful to have, at this moment with the technology where we are, and things are moving so quickly that this might not be true by the end of the week.

254
00:23:57,430 --> 00:24:08,310
But from what I've seen at this moment, it's really useful to have, let's say, a separate agent doing things like code review rather than letting the code builder self-supervise.

255
00:24:08,310 --> 00:24:14,751
And so you want this agent that's going to look at the code as an artifact and say, "Is it meeting my goals? Is it well-designed?

256
00:24:14,751 --> 00:24:17,150
Does it have good internal APIs?"

257
00:24:17,150 --> 00:24:21,990
All of these things that we think add up to good code maintainability over time.

258
00:24:21,990 --> 00:24:27,630
So a different agent, differently prompted, an agent with different goals, and that's what's important for the agents.

259
00:24:27,640 --> 00:24:32,911
Is it helpful to use a different model for that? I think tentatively yes again.

260
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:41,390
I think we've seen some good indications that doing that code review with models of different sizes or models of different capabilities is useful,

261
00:24:41,390 --> 00:24:49,310
but I wouldn't say that's anywhere near as useful as the sort of fundamental multi-agent architecture of saying, "Hey,

262
00:24:49,310 --> 00:25:00,550
I want my code review to be done by an agent whose goal it is to drive for great code quality rather than the agent whose goal it is to implement the specification and build the code initially.

263
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:06,310
Earlier we talked about how you came up with this methodology.

264
00:25:06,310 --> 00:25:14,630
Do you happen to have a story where you can compare and contrast projects that were built wipe coded versus falling a spec driven methodology?

265
00:25:14,640 --> 00:25:26,950
I don't know if we've done any kind of side-by-side between wipe coding and spec driven development.

266
00:25:26,950 --> 00:25:32,030
But what we have found is let me say that Amazon has a very, let's say,

267
00:25:32,030 --> 00:25:43,030
service-oriented architecture, kind of microservice architecture, and we have literally thousands of services within the company that provide certain things.

268
00:25:43,030 --> 00:25:48,350
And as we've adopted AI, what we've heard from the teams that own those services is they really started with this kind of wipe coding.

269
00:25:48,350 --> 00:25:53,350
And what they were finding was they would pull their code base into an IDE.

270
00:25:53,350 --> 00:25:55,790
They would prompt for a change.

271
00:25:55,790 --> 00:26:03,910
Often they would see that change successfully implemented, but they would see also a regression.

272
00:26:03,910 --> 00:26:14,350
At the same time, the AI would kind of forget one of the things, properties about the code, and be like, "Well, I've optimized this, but that breaks the API and makes the service not work anymore."

273
00:26:14,350 --> 00:26:22,350
And so what we're seeing internally at Amazon is going through the process of I've got the service code.

274
00:26:22,350 --> 00:26:27,950
I'm going to extract from that a specification, even if it is a kind of method-by-method code-to-text process,

275
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:39,230
and then I'm going to use that specification in a spec driven development flow makes that agent that is building the next feature much better at not undoing goodness of the past.

276
00:26:39,230 --> 00:26:45,030
And a separate agent that's building tests much better at thinking about which tests should it build.

277
00:26:45,030 --> 00:26:52,990
And so I don't think we've done a lot of side-by-side, but I think what we have found as teams have embraced specification driven development,

278
00:26:52,990 --> 00:27:04,230
that specification abstraction is a step that helps them move a lot faster because it stops this two step forward, one step back dynamic that can happen with existing code bases with wipe coding.

279
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:07,470
I think that's a good point, and we'll come back to that.

280
00:27:07,470 --> 00:27:10,110
I just talked a little bit more about providing context.

281
00:27:10,120 --> 00:27:11,950
But before that, I'm curious.

282
00:27:11,950 --> 00:27:20,950
So spec driven is a methodology Amazon built Kiro, but I'm sure spec driven could be applied to other agentic ideas.

283
00:27:20,950 --> 00:27:23,591
Is that a fair assumption? Oh yeah, absolutely.

284
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:28,430
It's a methodology that you can use with any of the popular tools.

285
00:27:28,430 --> 00:27:35,830
It's not as ergonomic as we've tried to make it in Kiro, but it's a methodology with general goodness.

286
00:27:35,830 --> 00:27:40,511
So one we recommend to our teams who are building software, whatever tools they use. Thanks, Mike.

287
00:27:40,520 --> 00:27:44,030
Let's go back to providing the context.

288
00:27:44,040 --> 00:27:56,270
I think you brought up a good point is that unless the spec itself or what you told the LLM had the context of the code, there were always regressions.

289
00:27:56,270 --> 00:27:59,470
Can we generalize that?

290
00:27:59,470 --> 00:28:06,550
Is it then true that wipe coding is more effective for new projects or for open source projects?

291
00:28:06,560 --> 00:28:19,550
I don't know about open source, but I think we have seen, at least anecdotally, that if you build a code base with AI from the beginning, the AI finds that code easier to understand.

292
00:28:19,560 --> 00:28:28,750
Why that is, I don't really know, but anecdotally that is true from my experience and similar to what I hear from my colleagues.

293
00:28:28,750 --> 00:28:40,070
I'll also say that for me the easy and fun feeling of this kind of pure wipe coding works best in the kind of green field new project situation.

294
00:28:40,070 --> 00:28:50,310
But as soon as I have a significant existing code base that I'm working on, then I don't find I get as much of a benefit from that kind of interaction.

295
00:28:50,310 --> 00:28:55,110
And so yeah, I would say that that wipe coding flow is just maximally awesome and

296
00:28:55,110 --> 00:29:03,311
productive for getting from nothing to a great first prototype, but after that, it is less scalable. It kind of Peters out, right?

297
00:29:03,320 --> 00:29:09,950
Whereas the specification flow loads a little bit more work up front, and so there's no hiding that.

298
00:29:09,950 --> 00:29:19,470
But what you get out of that is a much more sustainable ongoing process, an ongoing process that is much easier to maintain,

299
00:29:19,470 --> 00:29:28,390
much easier to iterate on, much easier to add features to without regressions, much easier to get great test coverage, much easier to get great QA coverage.

300
00:29:28,390 --> 00:29:38,630
And so by loading a little bit more work, not a huge amount more work, but a little bit more work at the beginning, what you end up with is a process that doesn't drop off, right?

301
00:29:38,630 --> 00:29:41,310
A process that remains effective and productive over the long term.

302
00:29:41,320 --> 00:29:48,470
So in terms of context, you talked about extracting the context and having that provided as part of the spec.

303
00:29:48,470 --> 00:29:50,550
But, is that sufficient?

304
00:29:50,550 --> 00:29:52,230
I mean, the code base could be pretty large.

305
00:29:52,230 --> 00:29:55,311
You certainly Yeah. do that all the time.

306
00:29:55,311 --> 00:29:58,430
What other ways are there of providing context?

307
00:29:58,430 --> 00:30:02,830
I really like that question, and I think for two reasons.

308
00:30:02,830 --> 00:30:10,390
Like, one of them is I think in a lot of ways 2026 is the year of thinking about context management for AI agents.

309
00:30:10,390 --> 00:30:14,150
It really has become one of the most important problems in the world of agent building.

310
00:30:14,150 --> 00:30:20,150
So, this is something that everyone across the industry is going a bunch of thought to and bunch of investment in this year.

311
00:30:20,150 --> 00:30:25,670
But then if we think about, okay, how do we think about that in this particular case of software building?

312
00:30:25,670 --> 00:30:35,750
And what's really interesting to me is so many of the techniques that we've developed to make software and code bases understandable to humans also work really well in this context, right?

313
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:48,271
Like, we have always, you know, from the early days of kind of structured programming kind of going all the way back to, you know, 60 years ago thought about interfaces? How do we build modularity?

314
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:51,830
How do we extract functionality into libraries?

315
00:30:51,830 --> 00:30:54,470
How do we extract functionality behind APIs?

316
00:30:54,470 --> 00:31:01,870
You know, whether that's a library API or a local service API or remote microservice API.

317
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:03,790
You know, we have made software modular.

318
00:31:03,800 --> 00:31:06,390
We've we've built APIs.

319
00:31:06,390 --> 00:31:09,751
We've built uh contracts. We've built protocols, right?

320
00:31:09,760 --> 00:31:24,390
These are all ways to take a extremely complex piece of software, limit the interactions, and allow us as humans to reason about the way that that, you know, piece of software works.

321
00:31:24,390 --> 00:31:26,910
And in a lot of ways,

322
00:31:26,910 --> 00:31:35,550
what makes the difference between long-term successful software and unsuccessful long-term software is their ability to be built in a way that

323
00:31:35,560 --> 00:31:43,950
they can evolve without every change being able to change everything about the way they work.

324
00:31:43,950 --> 00:31:48,950
And that's been what has driven the popularity of of service-oriented and microservice architecture.

325
00:31:48,950 --> 00:31:54,070
What's driven the popularity of libraries and modularization for the whole of the history of software.

326
00:31:54,070 --> 00:32:05,751
And so, we can kind of think about that, well, in AI, also what we need to manage the context and help an AI understand our code base is modularity. Is good design.

327
00:32:05,751 --> 00:32:08,710
Is good APIs.

328
00:32:08,710 --> 00:32:14,190
Is good documentation of the APIs and their contracts and and what they mean and what you can assume and what you can depend on.

329
00:32:14,200 --> 00:32:19,551
And then once you've gotten that, then you can layer on top things like semantic search. Right?

330
00:32:19,551 --> 00:32:22,030
I'm looking for the library method that does this.

331
00:32:22,030 --> 00:32:24,669
Well, here it is.

332
00:32:24,669 --> 00:32:29,310
And so, I don't have to understand everything about the entire system.

333
00:32:29,310 --> 00:32:33,990
I can say this library call takes these inputs and has this effect on the system.

334
00:32:33,990 --> 00:32:39,070
And so, I can reason, you know, more locally about the behavior of the system.

335
00:32:39,070 --> 00:32:45,669
And so, for me, kind of context management for for software agents is very much like context management for

336
00:32:45,669 --> 00:32:51,669
human developers is converting the impossible problem at scale of reasoning about every line of code in the system and

337
00:32:51,669 --> 00:33:00,990
their interactions into this more local reasoning thing of like, can I think about this one library call, this one change?

338
00:33:00,990 --> 00:33:12,790
And the more that we can design our software to make changes as local as possible, the less context you need to successfully make changes, the more you can well test changes, and the more productive you can be.

339
00:33:12,790 --> 00:33:21,910
And I think that's true of of AI agents just as it has been true of humans over you know, six plus decades of of software engineering practice.

340
00:33:21,910 --> 00:33:24,510
So, you mentioned one way of I guess two ways.

341
00:33:24,510 --> 00:33:35,310
One was extracting context into the spec and then semantic search where you potentially have to create vectors for bunch of code in your code base.

342
00:33:35,310 --> 00:33:40,550
What else is there that you've experimented with?

343
00:33:40,550 --> 00:33:45,070
Do you have any guidance on somebody that is getting started now in this area, what they should do?

344
00:33:45,080 --> 00:33:55,750
Yeah, I mean, I think there's some really interesting work going on around indexing code bases and around code understanding and, you know, extracting understanding from code bases.

345
00:33:55,750 --> 00:34:01,550
But what I would encourage people to do who are starting out and

346
00:34:01,550 --> 00:34:12,431
are getting into this kind of coding is to you know, think about software design and think about internal interfaces. You know, think about types.

347
00:34:12,440 --> 00:34:15,830
Think about APIs.

348
00:34:15,830 --> 00:34:22,510
And think about building your software in a way that well encapsulates the functionality of each component.

349
00:34:22,510 --> 00:34:32,110
And that makes it much easier when you say, you know, to the AI or you you change the specification to find the lines of code that are going to do things, right?

350
00:34:32,120 --> 00:34:42,070
Like, it's you know, hey, UI.ts you know, TS or whatever rather than, hey, this is just spread out through my code base.

351
00:34:42,070 --> 00:34:43,869
I can go and find the lines of code.

352
00:34:43,869 --> 00:34:46,510
I can go and change the lines of code, and they are as local as possible.

353
00:34:46,520 --> 00:35:01,310
And so, you know, one of the things to maybe optimize for and pay attention to as you're getting started with this is you look at I'm going to make a change to my specification, maybe to add a small feature.

354
00:35:01,310 --> 00:35:07,590
How many of the modules of my system does the the AI need to touch to make that to make that happen?

355
00:35:07,590 --> 00:35:16,870
And if it has to touch every module in my system, well, then I've got a design problem that is going to make my AI-driven development less productive over time.

356
00:35:16,870 --> 00:35:24,950
You know, if it can make a a small feature change by touching, you know, one or two of the modules in my one or two of the libraries,

357
00:35:24,950 --> 00:35:34,590
one or two of the services, however I affected things, well, then I've got a design that is likely to work over the long term and and scale up well without,

358
00:35:34,590 --> 00:35:41,349
you know, blowing up context windows, without requiring, you know, sophisticated techniques to index and so on.

359
00:35:41,349 --> 00:35:43,510
And so, if I'm seeing that number go up,

360
00:35:43,510 --> 00:35:51,470
that kind of complete or number of modules I have to to change to build a a feature or or to add some capability go up over time,

361
00:35:51,470 --> 00:35:58,470
maybe that's time to step back and then work with the AI to do some refactoring, to kind of tease apart modules, to tease apart APIs.

362
00:35:58,470 --> 00:36:10,349
And the good news is that, you know, AIs and you know, these agents and especially, you know, once you've invested in great testing, they're extremely powerful at doing, you know, refactoring tasks.

363
00:36:10,359 --> 00:36:18,071
Let's talk about your comment on blowing up context windows. When does that happen?

364
00:36:18,080 --> 00:36:25,590
And a related question is, when is there too much context which actually harms outcomes?

365
00:36:25,600 --> 00:36:35,910
You know, this varies model to model, and it has become less of a problem as we've had, you know, some slightly better you know, better model capabilities. But it is still a real problem.

366
00:36:35,920 --> 00:36:39,070
Right?

367
00:36:39,070 --> 00:36:42,110
And so, you know, if I go into a coffee shop and they say, "What would you like to drink?"

368
00:36:42,110 --> 00:36:43,910
And I say, "Well, I would like a latte."

369
00:36:43,910 --> 00:36:52,310
And by the way, it's also 18° outside and the Seahawks won yesterday and, you know, not only is that going to waste a bunch of time,

370
00:36:52,310 --> 00:36:58,790
but it is going to confuse the person who, you know, it's like, "Why is this relevant? Why are you telling me all of these things?"

371
00:36:58,790 --> 00:37:02,870
Is there some way that you want me to customize your drink based on all of this extra information?

372
00:37:02,880 --> 00:37:05,030
And so, it confuses humans.

373
00:37:05,030 --> 00:37:06,750
And I think, you know, almost by analogy.

374
00:37:06,750 --> 00:37:18,430
And it's a little bit risky to reason by analogy with AI, but almost by analogy, putting a bunch of irrelevant stuff into a context window makes those AI outcomes worse.

375
00:37:18,430 --> 00:37:24,230
And so, you know, this is where, you know, context management becomes so important.

376
00:37:24,230 --> 00:37:25,990
And there are two aspects of it.

377
00:37:25,990 --> 00:37:30,190
One of them is all of that design and modularity stuff that I I just talked about.

378
00:37:30,190 --> 00:37:39,150
And the other is the AI tool, whether that's Kiro or one of the many other AI-powered development tools, have to do, you know, have to manage what's in the context window,

379
00:37:39,150 --> 00:37:46,870
making sure that there is enough context to give the AI the power to at least discover what it should be doing.

380
00:37:46,870 --> 00:37:48,510
But there shouldn't be too much context.

381
00:37:48,510 --> 00:37:55,510
If it contains a bunch of irrelevant noise, we're going to have a worse outcome.

382
00:37:55,520 --> 00:38:00,070
You know, and so, if you think about like, what do agents do?

383
00:38:00,070 --> 00:38:06,670
Well, one of the things that AI agents do is build their own context window through a process of discovery.

384
00:38:06,670 --> 00:38:18,230
They'll go and read that file, search this index, search, you know, use this MCP tool to do a semantic search over the documentation, and try and build their own context window.

385
00:38:18,230 --> 00:38:20,510
And again, one of the things that

386
00:38:20,510 --> 00:38:29,110
makes agent development challenging is making sure they build their context window as reliably as possible,

387
00:38:29,110 --> 00:38:33,470
containing as high a ratio of relevant to to irrelevant information as possible.

388
00:38:33,470 --> 00:38:35,230
And so, there are kinds of three things there.

389
00:38:35,230 --> 00:38:43,190
There's how do I build my code base and maintain my code base to make it kind of context friendly for humans and agents.

390
00:38:43,190 --> 00:38:48,470
How do I as somebody who's building development tools think about, you know, context management.

391
00:38:48,470 --> 00:38:56,430
And then how do I as a developer of agents more generally think about guiding those agents to build their context windows effectively.

392
00:38:56,430 --> 00:39:01,230
And then, you know, the tools I give to the agents, what do they return?

393
00:39:01,230 --> 00:39:02,910
the right stuff?

394
00:39:02,910 --> 00:39:08,550
Do they give the agent the ability to request only the facts it needs and so on?

395
00:39:08,550 --> 00:39:19,950
What about the process to make sure that the way you provide the context, the source of that is if it's not the code directly is kept up to date.

396
00:39:19,950 --> 00:39:23,990
Is that something outside of the dev process or it's kind of inbuilt there?

397
00:39:23,990 --> 00:39:28,230
Yeah, you know, and this has been, again, not a new problem in the world of software.

398
00:39:28,230 --> 00:39:30,950
How do I I keep my documentation up to date?

399
00:39:30,950 --> 00:39:41,230
And you know, one of the real challenges of the kind of pre-AI era is is how do I keep my requirements and specification up to date?

400
00:39:41,230 --> 00:39:48,550
And specification-driven development has made made that much easier by making, you know, everything flow down from the specification and,

401
00:39:48,560 --> 00:39:54,910
you know, has helped with that specification freshness problem a huge amount.

402
00:39:54,910 --> 00:39:56,950
But there's still a documentation freshness problem.

403
00:39:56,950 --> 00:40:01,510
Well, how do I keep my documentation up to date?

404
00:40:01,510 --> 00:40:13,230
What I did in the previous AI era is, you know, I would spend time from my development tool or technical writers who would, you know, look for changes in the code and go off and update documentation.

405
00:40:13,230 --> 00:40:19,991
Now, I can fact ex- you know, very effectively accelerate that process with AI and have, you know,

406
00:40:19,991 --> 00:40:26,750
an agent in my kind of set of agents that's doing my helping me with my software development process that

407
00:40:26,750 --> 00:40:33,510
is going off and keeping my documentation up to date, keeping my read me up to date, keeping those API docs up to date.

408
00:40:33,510 --> 00:40:41,350
And, you know, that feeds back into the next round of my process where I want to make a change. How do I know that that API doc is up to date?

409
00:40:41,350 --> 00:40:47,950
Because I have continuously kept it up to date as part of my software development flow.

410
00:40:47,960 --> 00:40:56,270
You know, and so even when I'm vibe coding, even when I'm not doing the kind of specification development process,

411
00:40:56,270 --> 00:41:03,470
I always make sure that my prompts or my steering document are directing the agents to keep a read me up to date or

412
00:41:03,470 --> 00:41:16,831
keep a piece of documentation up to date, both for my reference as a human and my customer's reference as they use my software, but also for the reference of the agent itself as it makes the next set of steps. Thanks, Mark.

413
00:41:16,831 --> 00:41:26,150
And now I'd like to spend a little bit of time on how do you make sure that the configuration and the model itself is effective?

414
00:41:26,150 --> 00:41:33,710
Initially, you roll this out to the team and now the core group that's maintaining the config,

415
00:41:33,710 --> 00:41:37,950
the contacts, the different workflows that somebody has to do, how do they keep them up to date?

416
00:41:37,950 --> 00:41:40,910
So, let's say there's a new model that comes along.

417
00:41:40,910 --> 00:41:44,990
How do you make sure that it hasn't regressed the flow in any form or fashion?

418
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:51,790
Yeah, and so the I think the formal answer to that is, you know, we have an evaluation process.

419
00:41:51,790 --> 00:41:59,830
And that's where, you know, we will take an agentic workload and probably starting off offline,

420
00:41:59,830 --> 00:42:09,670
run a bunch of examples through a new model or a new configuration or a new prompt and compare the success of the old versus the new.

421
00:42:09,670 --> 00:42:17,830
And that could be with a human looking at that, that could be looking at are the tests passing, it could be looking at acceptance rate,

422
00:42:17,830 --> 00:42:25,830
or it could be using a pattern like LLM as a judge where it uses another agent essentially to rate the agent outputs and say these ones are better and these ones are worse.

423
00:42:25,830 --> 00:42:35,910
The next step of that and and and the one that I think is is actually most powerful is online evaluation where, okay,

424
00:42:35,910 --> 00:42:40,230
I'm going to start experimenting with this new model in production, kind of AB testing.

425
00:42:40,230 --> 00:42:42,350
I'm going to send it 5% of traffic.

426
00:42:42,350 --> 00:42:44,270
I'm going to send it 10% of traffic.

427
00:42:44,270 --> 00:42:54,590
And then I'm going to feed back all of my success signals, whether those are LLM as a judge, whether that's human feedback, whether that's latency information,

428
00:42:54,590 --> 00:42:58,750
test success rate, to look at am I doing better with this new version?

429
00:42:58,750 --> 00:43:04,030
If it looks like I'm doing better, if those those initial experiments are looking good, well, I'm going to ramp that up.

430
00:43:04,030 --> 00:43:10,190
Eventually, I'm going to get that up to 100% and then I'm going to be ready to do, you know, do the next experiment.

431
00:43:10,200 --> 00:43:18,790
And then continuously in production, I'm going to continuously be evaluating the outcomes of my agent.

432
00:43:18,790 --> 00:43:26,830
Again, the metrics I use for that depend on the task and I'm going to know over time is my success rate good?

433
00:43:26,830 --> 00:43:35,551
And, you know, if my success rate suddenly changes for whatever reason or trends downwards, well, then I need to understand why that is. Maybe my tasks have changed.

434
00:43:35,551 --> 00:43:37,870
Maybe my customer's needs have changed.

435
00:43:37,870 --> 00:43:40,150
Maybe I have some bad piece of context.

436
00:43:40,150 --> 00:43:46,470
Maybe that read me contains some false, you know, information that's that's leading my agents off in the wrong direction.

437
00:43:46,480 --> 00:43:57,230
But, you know, the really key thing here is to have from the agent builder's perspective and and here I'm I'm not talking about, you know, the the IDE user,

438
00:43:57,230 --> 00:44:01,349
they don't need to really worry about this stuff, but me as an agent builder,

439
00:44:01,349 --> 00:44:10,550
I need to think about how do I have a robust set of evaluations so I can run a really data-driven improvement process around around agents,

440
00:44:10,550 --> 00:44:17,349
around the models, around the prompts, around the context, around the way the context is managed.

441
00:44:17,349 --> 00:44:28,390
And the more I do that, well, the better a quality product I can offer to my customers, the more reliable it will be, but it also helps me do things like, hey, I'm doing something with a really big model today.

442
00:44:28,390 --> 00:44:30,710
Can I lower my latency by using a smaller model?

443
00:44:30,720 --> 00:44:34,750
Answering that question in isolation, man, that's next to impossible.

444
00:44:34,760 --> 00:44:39,230
Answering that question when I have a robust set of evaluators is much easier.

445
00:44:39,240 --> 00:44:49,670
And so, this is a process for agent builders, but it really goes back to the beginning of the whole question of you know, what are we trying to achieve?

446
00:44:49,680 --> 00:44:51,710
What is the outcome that we want?

447
00:44:51,710 --> 00:44:54,990
You know, what do our customers need and what does our business need?

448
00:44:55,000 --> 00:44:58,830
What is the outcome and then what is the success rate?

449
00:44:58,830 --> 00:45:03,070
But maybe just specifically what kind of things should team be measuring?

450
00:45:03,070 --> 00:45:07,030
Should they be measuring bug rates?

451
00:45:07,030 --> 00:45:11,390
You know, is there human correction needed during human code reviews?

452
00:45:11,390 --> 00:45:15,670
Do should they be measuring the divergence of the code from the spec?

453
00:45:15,670 --> 00:45:19,830
You know, could you share some of those thoughts on what metrics team should focus on?

454
00:45:19,840 --> 00:45:23,830
Again, like the most important ones are the end-to-end ones.

455
00:45:23,830 --> 00:45:25,870
You know, are customers seeing bugs in production?

456
00:45:25,870 --> 00:45:29,870
Are customers seeing regressions in production?

457
00:45:29,870 --> 00:45:35,070
Are, you know, are customers happy with the in-production performance? Now, those are the most important metrics.

458
00:45:35,070 --> 00:45:36,870
They're also the hardest to measure.

459
00:45:36,870 --> 00:45:43,830
They're also the ones with the highest risk because we want to catch things before customers are reporting bugs in production.

460
00:45:43,830 --> 00:45:49,270
And so, you know, we're we're going to choose some earlier in the process kind of proxies for those metrics.

461
00:45:49,270 --> 00:45:55,190
And that's where, you know, am I seeing failures in regression tests, for example?

462
00:45:55,190 --> 00:46:01,110
What is the test rate of failures in regression tests?

463
00:46:01,110 --> 00:46:07,070
Am I seeing a Yeah, you know, if I'm using human code review, am I seeing a good pass rate for that human code review?

464
00:46:07,070 --> 00:46:11,590
If I'm using a separate code review agent, am I seeing a good pass rate for that code review agent?

465
00:46:11,590 --> 00:46:15,510
Is it giving me good success?

466
00:46:15,510 --> 00:46:19,990
If I'm using a, you know, pen testing agent like, you know,

467
00:46:19,990 --> 00:46:29,550
the one that we announced at at re:Invent that does kind of last-mile security testing, is that finding, you know, security bugs before things go into production?

468
00:46:29,550 --> 00:46:39,510
And so, if I'm seeing bugs being caught at my sort of last pre-production milestone, I know something's not going well earlier on in my software development process.

469
00:46:39,510 --> 00:46:45,310
And that could be an inadequate kind of early in the software life cycle testing.

470
00:46:45,310 --> 00:46:47,870
It could be a symptom of bad design and interfaces.

471
00:46:47,870 --> 00:46:52,349
It could maybe be a symptom of some bad implementation choices I need to go and fix.

472
00:46:52,349 --> 00:47:02,270
Or it could be a misalignment between the requirements of the tests that are going into the test and the requirements that are going into the software building process.

473
00:47:02,270 --> 00:47:12,870
You know, and so if if one of these things says you need to return a a result to the customer within 10 milliseconds and the other one says 10 seconds, well, obviously I'm going to have failures.

474
00:47:12,880 --> 00:47:22,710
And so, there's this tension in these metrics of the most valuable metrics are the ones all the way at the end of my software development life cycle because

475
00:47:22,720 --> 00:47:35,590
they are what I really care about, they're what my customers see, but that's the most expensive place and so I want to push as much of that, you know, catching of things in those metrics sort of left in my pipeline as possible.

476
00:47:35,600 --> 00:47:39,751
Again, classic idea of software engineering, right? Catch bugs early.

477
00:47:39,760 --> 00:47:45,510
You know, we've been software engineers have been saying that for for four or five decades and that hasn't changed.

478
00:47:45,520 --> 00:47:48,670
Thanks, Mark.

479
00:47:48,670 --> 00:47:51,270
Now, let's talk more about how do you roll this out to the team?

480
00:47:51,280 --> 00:47:54,030
Are there any prerequisites?

481
00:47:54,030 --> 00:47:56,310
Is there a mind shift that has to happen?

482
00:47:56,310 --> 00:48:00,830
Or do you just have a meeting, you introduce the tool and tell everyone, hey, go use it?

483
00:48:00,840 --> 00:48:03,030
Yeah, I mean, I think that varies.

484
00:48:03,030 --> 00:48:08,671
I think the biggest prerequisite is almost cultural, right? Too.

485
00:48:08,671 --> 00:48:21,190
So, what we've seen with with adoption of of new development practices is you need a team that has, you know, great understanding of their customer and business and high standards, right?

486
00:48:21,190 --> 00:48:24,230
You can't accept, I don't know, slop, I guess is the term that people use these days, right?

487
00:48:24,230 --> 00:48:29,750
We don't want that in our software processes.

488
00:48:29,750 --> 00:48:36,510
But we also need folks and see the fastest transformation that have great curiosity, who are really interested in picking up new things and,

489
00:48:36,520 --> 00:48:42,830
you know, great bias for action of like, hey, we're going to go off and and try stuff and and experiment with stuff.

490
00:48:42,830 --> 00:48:49,910
And so, it's that tension, I think, within teams that I've seen really drive success here.

491
00:48:49,910 --> 00:49:02,390
Is the teams are like, well, we're going to go off and we're going to try new things and we're really interested in learning and trying and adopting new tools, but at the same time we're not going to settle for bad outcomes for our customers, for our business, for our engineering teams.

492
00:49:02,400 --> 00:49:08,510
And, you know, when you put those two things together, I think that's really where where magic happens.

493
00:49:08,520 --> 00:49:21,030
You know, beyond that kind of cultural goodness, there is a, you know, we also shouldn't expect that teams can pick up a new tool and know immediately how to use them well, right?

494
00:49:21,040 --> 00:49:31,150
You you need this kind of communication and sharing of saying, hey, we've tried it this way, these are the things we learned, you know, these are the things that we want to pass on to you.

495
00:49:31,150 --> 00:49:35,470
You want to bake those into communication that that that you have between your teams.

496
00:49:35,480 --> 00:49:40,790
And then if you're building tools, you want to bake those lessons into your tools.

497
00:49:40,790 --> 00:49:50,910
And so again, I think another piece of success is making sure that as a team learns how to use the tools better or to improve the tools, the whole organization learns.

498
00:49:50,910 --> 00:49:55,110
And ideally, you know, the whole world learns and we can kind of drive everything forward.

499
00:49:55,120 --> 00:50:06,310
And sometimes that's big learnings of saying, "Hey, we want to introduce specification driven development because we've learned that that scales better than, you know, prompt by prompt vibe coding."

500
00:50:06,310 --> 00:50:13,990
And sometimes it's little things of saying, "Hey, we found that indexing our code this way or structuring our code this way works really well.

501
00:50:13,990 --> 00:50:18,630
We found, for example, concretely internally at Amazon that building AI,

502
00:50:18,630 --> 00:50:25,470
you know, using AI powered development with a language like Rust with its strong type system and a bunch of compile time checking is really,

503
00:50:25,480 --> 00:50:33,110
really useful and helps accelerate development by catching bugs earlier in the pipeline."

504
00:50:33,110 --> 00:50:37,070
And so that's a message that we want to send out to our teams and say, you know, Rust works well.

505
00:50:37,080 --> 00:50:39,590
TypeScript works pretty well.

506
00:50:39,590 --> 00:50:43,710
You know, the type annotations in Python, they're worth investing in.

507
00:50:43,710 --> 00:50:51,310
And, you know, it's so it is that spreading of learning across an organization that is is super important.

508
00:50:51,310 --> 00:50:59,030
And then maybe the the last thought on that is, "Wow, you know, no surprise to anybody, this whole world of AI is is moving so fast, right?

509
00:50:59,030 --> 00:51:04,190
There are new techniques, there are new tools there pretty seemingly every day and every week."

510
00:51:04,200 --> 00:51:14,310
One of the things that you need to balance for success is, you know, you want your teams to be paying attention to those new tools, to be curious about them.

511
00:51:14,310 --> 00:51:19,230
But you also can't afford to go and chase, you know, every week's trend day-to-day.

512
00:51:19,230 --> 00:51:25,710
And so there's a balance to be found there of we want to adopt new stuff, we want to adopt the best things quickly,

513
00:51:25,710 --> 00:51:34,510
but we don't want to spend all of our time just thrashing on trying the latest, you know, the latest and greatest or or most fashionable tool of the moment.

514
00:51:34,510 --> 00:51:37,870
So yeah, there's a lot to put together into organizational success.

515
00:51:37,880 --> 00:51:46,110
So certainly, of course, curiosity is critical but bias for action seems to be more important based on your comment.

516
00:51:46,120 --> 00:51:54,351
Though with this specifically, with AI specifically, there's a little bit of fear as well. How do you address that?

517
00:51:54,360 --> 00:51:56,950
What kind of fear? Can you say more about the fears?

518
00:51:56,960 --> 00:52:07,470
I mean, with AI doing more and more, is different things expected of the developer or less expected of the developer as we proceed on this journey?

519
00:52:07,480 --> 00:52:16,630
Well, you know, I think that the job of software development and, you know, more broadly the job of of software engineering is changing.

520
00:52:16,630 --> 00:52:24,710
And our jobs are going to be very different in, you know, 2, 5, 10 years from from the jobs that they are today.

521
00:52:24,710 --> 00:52:31,030
And, you know, with any major change, there is going to come uncertainty.

522
00:52:31,030 --> 00:52:40,790
People are going to to think about, you know, "What does this change mean for me and and for the reasons that I went into this field and and the things that I love about this kind of work."

523
00:52:40,790 --> 00:52:43,710
And, you know, I'm super optimistic about that, right?

524
00:52:43,710 --> 00:52:52,030
I think AI powered development is freeing us up to spend more time on the things that matter.

525
00:52:52,040 --> 00:53:02,590
And, you know, whether those things that matter are, you know, being closer to customers, being closer to the business, really understanding the big picture of the systems we're building,

526
00:53:02,590 --> 00:53:11,550
or, you know, getting really deep on the you know, various parts of of the design, getting really deep on protocols, getting really deep on efficiency.

527
00:53:11,550 --> 00:53:18,470
And, you know, less time on the sort of undifferentiated busy work of building.

528
00:53:18,470 --> 00:53:25,470
So yeah, I mean, it's a time of really fast change in the industry and I think that is always going to come with some level of anxiety.

529
00:53:25,470 --> 00:53:35,270
You know, I'm super optimistic that it is going to, you know, free us up to do a a job that is is more fulfilling and more valuable.

530
00:53:35,270 --> 00:53:42,190
And, you know, we've seen over decades, you know, how powerful a economic force software has been.

531
00:53:42,190 --> 00:53:49,670
And over decades and, you know, the whole lifetime of software, it has been supply constrained.

532
00:53:49,670 --> 00:53:54,270
The the economic impact of of software has been constrained by how much great software we can build.

533
00:53:54,270 --> 00:54:01,270
And, you know, I strongly believe that if we can drive down the cost of software development with AI tools by 10x,

534
00:54:01,280 --> 00:54:09,070
there's going to be at least 10x economic impact of software, if not 100x economic impact of software.

535
00:54:09,070 --> 00:54:16,270
And so, you know, I think software and and software engineering is going to be even more important in the future than it is today.

536
00:54:16,280 --> 00:54:17,910
But it is going to be a different job.

537
00:54:17,920 --> 00:54:23,550
It's going to have, you know, different things and and we're going to need to optimize in in different ways.

538
00:54:23,550 --> 00:54:29,110
And I think it's very reasonable for people to feel challenged by that.

539
00:54:29,110 --> 00:54:35,430
I think it's very reasonable for people to reflect and say, "Hey, what does this this mean for me?"

540
00:54:35,430 --> 00:54:43,590
You know, I can't say anything other than personally, as someone who's been excited and passionate about technology, I think the future is is just super exciting.

541
00:54:43,600 --> 00:54:45,670
Thanks, Mark.

542
00:54:45,670 --> 00:54:48,710
So starting to wrap up now, a couple of questions.

543
00:54:48,710 --> 00:54:53,630
What about the steps beyond the development of the software?

544
00:54:53,630 --> 00:54:56,750
What about deployment, incident management, etc.?

545
00:54:56,750 --> 00:55:02,310
Is that tied to anything that you're thinking of with Kiro or, you know, beyond that in AWS?

546
00:55:02,320 --> 00:55:17,150
So, you know, at re:Invent back in December, we announced our our DevOps agent, which is an agent that is designed for, you know, doing these DevOps things in the cloud, helping out with incidents, helping out with tickets, helping out with infrastructure as a service maintenance, and so on.

547
00:55:17,150 --> 00:55:20,590
So yeah, another part of the work that software teams do.

548
00:55:20,600 --> 00:55:31,150
We announced our security agent, which, you know, is for for helping out with that that pen testing and and other parts of the kind of security testing life cycle.

549
00:55:31,160 --> 00:55:40,950
You know, we have AWS Transform, which is a set of agents for, you know, taking moving systems from one implementation to another,

550
00:55:40,950 --> 00:55:47,470
you know, whether that is small patching tasks like, "Hey, I want to to pick up the latest version of Java."

551
00:55:47,470 --> 00:55:55,510
to major software kind of re-architectures of, you know, "I want to get off a mainframe and get into the cloud."

552
00:55:55,510 --> 00:55:59,710
And so, you know, it is vastly beyond just the work of building software.

553
00:55:59,710 --> 00:56:08,430
We're going to use agents to accelerate the deployment of software, to simplify the operations of software, to simplify, you know, software maintenance.

554
00:56:08,430 --> 00:56:10,790
And that's a huge one, right?

555
00:56:10,790 --> 00:56:21,911
I don't I don't know many software engineers who are excited about, you know, making sure I can migrate from Java 11 to Java 17 or or what it 21 or whatever the latest one is these days. But it's critical.

556
00:56:21,911 --> 00:56:26,590
It's critical work for security, for efficiency, for all of these, you know, other things.

557
00:56:26,590 --> 00:56:29,550
And so, you know, that's going to be a big one.

558
00:56:29,550 --> 00:56:36,910
The ability to change back-end implementations much more easily and with lower risk is is huge for engineering teams, right?

559
00:56:36,920 --> 00:56:40,349
Like Hey, I have all of this legacy. I built these things.

560
00:56:40,349 --> 00:56:43,349
Maybe I wrote all this code in Fortran.

561
00:56:43,349 --> 00:56:48,470
It is the crown jewel of my organization because it's the only expression of my business logic.

562
00:56:48,470 --> 00:56:50,349
You know, how do I bring that into a modern implementation?

563
00:56:50,349 --> 00:56:52,510
How do I bring that into a modern architecture?

564
00:56:52,520 --> 00:56:56,550
Again, you know, this is something that AI is really going to power for us.

565
00:56:56,560 --> 00:56:59,750
Testing, validation, UI testing.

566
00:56:59,750 --> 00:57:04,870
You know, there's so much of of the process around software.

567
00:57:04,870 --> 00:57:07,470
You know, even updating tickets, all of these things.

568
00:57:07,480 --> 00:57:17,430
I think you know, these are areas that we're investing in heavily in AWS because, you know, we have paid a bunch of attention to where,

569
00:57:17,430 --> 00:57:25,670
you know, software developers both internally and in our customers actually spending their time, software teams and organizations are spending their time.

570
00:57:25,670 --> 00:57:27,630
But they're not as shiny and cool as as software building.

571
00:57:27,630 --> 00:57:32,750
And so, you know, all the buzz is around, you know, here's a thing that helps me write more code more quickly.

572
00:57:32,750 --> 00:57:41,470
And I think that's okay, but really the big picture is here's something that allows me as an software organization,

573
00:57:41,470 --> 00:57:48,510
as a leader of a software organization, or to deliver value to my customers more quickly and more reliably.

574
00:57:48,510 --> 00:57:51,230
And that's going to take much more than just writing more code.

575
00:57:51,240 --> 00:57:54,910
But still, you are writing code as you're building these agents.

576
00:57:54,910 --> 00:57:57,750
You're writing code to build these agents.

577
00:57:57,750 --> 00:58:03,630
Is there special considerations for generating code for agents itself?

578
00:58:03,640 --> 00:58:10,990
Yeah, I think that is an area that is emerging.

579
00:58:10,990 --> 00:58:18,590
So if I look at the kind of first few generations of agent building, you know, very you know, a bunch of Python, bunch of kind of ad hoc techniques.

580
00:58:18,590 --> 00:58:21,110
It's been, you know, very valuable.

581
00:58:21,110 --> 00:58:27,270
But over time, you know, what's emerging is can we mix in tools like symbolic reasoning?

582
00:58:27,270 --> 00:58:30,349
Can we add more structure?

583
00:58:30,349 --> 00:58:34,750
You know, and so, for example, we we built this really cool set of features into Strands,

584
00:58:34,750 --> 00:58:40,430
which is our agent framework called Strands Steering,

585
00:58:40,430 --> 00:58:48,750
that uses a neuro-symbolic technique combining kind of models and symbolic reasoning to nudge agent trajectories in in the right direction.

586
00:58:48,760 --> 00:58:58,590
You know, we're seeing more tools that close that optimization and evaluation loop with improving tool descriptions and so on.

587
00:58:58,590 --> 00:59:03,510
You know, we're seeing tools like the policy that we built into Agent Core,

588
00:59:03,510 --> 00:59:12,510
which allows you to take a kind of plain text description of a security constraint or an operational constraint,

589
00:59:12,510 --> 00:59:20,550
turn that into a crisp piece of authorization code in a approvable language like Cedar, and apply those to all of the agents.

590
00:59:20,560 --> 00:59:27,750
And so, I think what we're going to learn and

591
00:59:27,750 --> 00:59:33,110
and are actively learning is how do we build software development practices and

592
00:59:33,110 --> 00:59:41,030
tools that go around agents that allow us to take advantage of the flexibility and power of agents, which is what we need, right?

593
00:59:41,030 --> 00:59:42,870
We want our agents to be flexible.

594
00:59:42,870 --> 00:59:55,630
We want them to almost be creative in their ability to solve problems, but we also need to do that in a way that is secure and meets the business needs and doesn't do things that are dangerous.

595
00:59:55,630 --> 00:59:57,550
And And so policy helps balance that.

596
00:59:57,550 --> 00:59:59,390
Strand steering helps balance that.

597
00:59:59,390 --> 01:00:05,990
You know, we also need to to reduce cost and and that's where things like model customization come in.

598
01:00:05,990 --> 01:00:17,350
And so agent building, you know, is in this this super exciting moment of a huge amount of both, you know, existing impact, but also a massive amount of innovation.

599
01:00:17,350 --> 01:00:19,070
And so,

600
01:00:19,070 --> 01:00:31,550
you know, I think what we're going to see is over the next few years building reliable agents is going to become even easier and easier and building cost-effective agents with great ROI is going to be easier.

601
01:00:31,550 --> 01:00:37,310
And so that's been our focus at AWS and it's certainly a focus across the industry of,

602
01:00:37,310 --> 01:00:45,911
you know, how do we take agent building from being this fairly ad hoc almost kind of bunch of spells to being a real, you know, a real engineering discipline. Thanks, Mark.

603
01:00:45,911 --> 01:00:55,990
So, we're running out of time, so I won't ask you about the stuff you mentioned a few times, the neuro-symbolic reasoning, but I'd love to get some references to add to our show notes for readers.

604
01:00:55,990 --> 01:00:58,590
Yeah, we'd love to share.

605
01:00:58,590 --> 01:01:02,350
Is there anything that we missed today that you'd like to spend a few minutes on?

606
01:01:02,360 --> 01:01:13,230
I think the overall message for me for software builders is if we think about code, you know, it encodes what it does, right?

607
01:01:13,230 --> 01:01:15,310
Like very, very crisply to computers.

608
01:01:15,310 --> 01:01:22,830
And we haven't historically been reliably in the habit as software builders of writing down why.

609
01:01:22,830 --> 01:01:25,070
Why is the code like this?

610
01:01:25,070 --> 01:01:34,910
And I think, you know, specification-driven development gets its power from making the why is the code like this explicit both to humans and to models.

611
01:01:34,910 --> 01:01:45,310
And so I think that is going to be this huge transformation we see in software is that the details of the how are going to maybe be less valuable.

612
01:01:45,310 --> 01:01:56,830
And the artifacts explaining the why, why is the software designed this way, what is it trying to achieve, who's it trying to achieve it for, is going to become more and more and more valuable.

613
01:01:56,830 --> 01:02:01,350
That is going to be true if you're doing specification development.

614
01:02:01,350 --> 01:02:03,470
I think it's true if you're doing vibe coding.

615
01:02:03,470 --> 01:02:12,990
And I think it's going to be true of, you know, whatever the next waves of agent-powered or AI-powered or even, you know, human development practices look like.

616
01:02:13,000 --> 01:02:15,270
Thanks, Mark.

617
01:02:15,270 --> 01:02:17,110
How can people contact you and keep up with your work?

618
01:02:17,120 --> 01:02:20,431
Check out my blog. That's brooker.co.za.

619
01:02:20,440 --> 01:02:26,310
And I'm on LinkedIn and X and and and various other social platforms.

620
01:02:26,310 --> 01:02:31,910
I love to share the work that me and my team are doing here at AWS on those platforms.

621
01:02:31,910 --> 01:02:37,270
You know, always, you know, love to hear thoughtful questions from folks and have great discussions about the future of software engineering.

622
01:02:37,280 --> 01:02:39,150
Thank you so much, Mark.

623
01:02:39,150 --> 01:02:42,910
This was a very interesting discussion on a very interesting topic.

624
01:02:42,920 --> 01:02:47,593
Great. Well, thank you and thanks for the opportunity and and tested conversation.

